  "plot_summary": "The issue picks up exactly where the last one left off, with Thor returning to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, fresh from his encounter with Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man.&nbsp; He returns to his office, changes to Don Blake and collapses from exhaustion, Jane helping him to a couch, all the while wondering why he doesn't propose marriage.\n\nMeanwhile, back in Asgard, Odin is still disappointed that he has not been able to get Thor to forget the mortal Jane Foster.&nbsp; Loki suggests that he only needs to meet a girl \"who is <i>more</i> beautiful, <i>more</i> desirable.\"&nbsp; Odin immediately realizes he's speaking of the Enchantress!&nbsp; Loki travels to see her, knowing that, despite her incredible beauty, she is cruel and ambitious.&nbsp; He presents the challenge of going to Earth and winning the heart of Don Blake.&nbsp; She accepts, \"for long have I wished to conquer the heart of handsome Thor!\"&nbsp; Loki knows that teaming up with the Enchantress would make them <i>quite</i> a formidable team, but they're too much alike and could never trust each other.\n\nSoon, the Enchantress arrives in New York and demands a wardrobe be made for her.&nbsp; She then goes to see Don Blake (who recognizes her) and manipulates him so that Jane walks into the room as the Enchantress kisses him.&nbsp; Jane storms out with Don hobbling after her.&nbsp; Enchantress is astonished at Thor's strength of character to resist her and returns to Asgard.&nbsp; She requires someone who can eliminate the mortal girl from the equation - the Executioner!&nbsp; He has always loved her, and she asks him to go to Earth and make sure that Don Blake is never able to set eyes on Jane Foster again!\n\nDon Blake turns to Thor to help in his search for Jane.&nbsp; He even barges into the police commissioner's office to request help in locating her, but he has a more pressing matter - there's an 8-foot tall guy with a battle axe spreading panic.&nbsp; Thor recognizes the description of the Executioner, who is out scanning the crowd for Jane...and <i>finds</i> her!&nbsp; He uses his battle axe to trap her in \"<i>Limbo</i>, the half-world between here and there!\"&nbsp; (Oh my, watch out for the Space Phantom from <i>Avengers</i> #2!)\n\nThor arrives in time to see her vanish and engages the Executioner.&nbsp; They battle for a couple of pages before the Executioner offers a deal.&nbsp; Thor will surrender his hammer to him, and in return, he will restore Jane.&nbsp; Thor reluctantly agrees, and Jane is restored.&nbsp; Thor hands him the hammer, which he now finds he cannot lift - what <i>good</i> is it then?\n\nBut suddenly, the voice of the Enchantress speaks, angry that he has made a deal instead of removing the girl as commissioned.&nbsp; She uses her sorcery to turn his limbs into tree branches, and the Enchantress then appears.&nbsp; The Executioner realizes that he needs Thor's help, and the hammer is restored to him (<i>thankfully all within 60 seconds</i>!).&nbsp; The Enchantress tries to turn the hammer into a serpent, but Thor reminds her that the hammer was \"forged Odin himself.&nbsp; No power in the universe, save <i>his</i>, can affect this enchanted weapon!\"&nbsp; And Thor reminds her that her beauty too can be resisted by \"the heart of a god!\"&nbsp; Thor then whirls his hammer around and banishes them.&nbsp; They are sent back to Asgard.\n\nSoon after, Don Blake catches up with Jane, who icily informs him that she's now waiting for Thor (hoping to make him jealous).&nbsp; All the while, Odin stares in amazement from above, not believing that they're <i>still</i> in love.&nbsp; He's <i>done</i> sitting around!&nbsp; \"By Asgard, my son may <i>not</i> love a mortal!&nbsp; Thor may not defy his father <i>Odin</i>!!&nbsp; The time for words is past!&nbsp; Now I must <i>act</i>!!\"\n\n(<i>Oh boy, Odin seems quite peeved!&nbsp; I'd hate to be Thor in the next issue!</i>)",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Definitely above reader level, with the introduction of two new characters, neither of which were given much time to be fleshed out much.&nbsp; But they were at least intriguing, and naturally, I'm sure we'll be seeing them again.\n\nWe're never told if Thor's full power was restored to him, having been halved a couple of issues ago.&nbsp; Since no mention was made of it, we must assume it was restored at the conclusion of the previous issue.&nbsp; (They might have at least <i>addressed</i> it in the text - I wonder if any astute readers of the past questioned this as well - I'll keep my eyes open for any letters to that effect.)\n\nThe story was functionally uneventful with the obligatory couple of action pages, for which the Executioner was perhaps invented for precisely the antagonist the story demanded.&nbsp; I mean, come <i>on</i> - Thor battling a goddess?&nbsp; That hardly seems right, so perhaps they brought him in to give Thor someone to fight with - this is Jack Kirby after all - there <i>has</i> to be <i>someone</i> duking it out every time!\n\nThe formula (now three issues old) of Odin staring at the action from above, looking to others to get his son's eyes off the mortal Jane Foster, seems to finally be broken, and Odin apparently is going to get involved directly!&nbsp; Interesting prospect.\n\nI do have to say the cover (which was more reminiscent of the 20 cent 1970's \"box\" covers) was quite impressive with Thor battling the two new foes on Bifrost, the Rainbow bridge against the backdrop of planets and outer space in general.&nbsp; I don't recall a cover with that type of grandiose setting.&nbsp; Even the far more famous <i>Silver Surfer</i> #4 cover doesn't have that much background depicted.&nbsp; (However, it's worth mentioning that the scene depicted on the cover never actually takes <i>place</i> in this story - ah well, a little creative license is to be expected, I guess.)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n\nThe issue has perhaps more detail of Asgard than we've been given before, and it's nice to see that realm taking shape - that it's more than just a mystical place where people sit around staring at what's going on in Midgard.\n\nNote that on page 12, Thor indicates that Mjolnir was \"forged by Odin himself.\"&nbsp; My recollection is different however, but I don't recall, so we'll definitely want to revisit this at some point.&nbsp; I know there is a story coming in a few issues which requires Thor to visit a steel mill in Pittsburg, so perhaps we'll get more background on the hammer then...",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:<i> </i></b> \"Thor's Mission to Mirmir!\"<b></b>\n\nThor arrives in the mountains of Asgard, where the dwarfs live and all the enchanted weapons of Asgard are forged.&nbsp; Here Thor is given a magic ship that flies through the air to take Thor wherever he needs to go.&nbsp; He heads to Mirmir, where he encounters a couple of perils (and once again indicates his \"hammer was forged by Odin himself\").&nbsp; Finally Thor approaches the one he seeks and hands him a branch \"from Yggdrasill, the tree of life!\" saying \"You know what must be done!\"\n\nWhen the unnamed god from Mirmir stirs the branch into some enchanted waters, two figures begin to take shape in Midgard below - the shapes of Aske and Embla, \"destined to start a new race, in the image of the immortals of Asgard!\"&nbsp; Thor heads home in his new magical ship, ready to be assigned his next task by Odin!\n\nWe learn in the Editor's Note that this tale was part of the Norse legends \"which deal with the birth of mankind and the days before the beginning of time.\""
  ],
